#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# sphere

#% text
# `sphere([x, y, z], r)` is a sphere of radius r centred at [x, y, z] in `graphics3d`.

#% text
# `sphere()` is the unit sphere at the origin; `sphere([c1, c2, ...], r)` several.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Coordinates are in the units of the graphic; the box around the scene follows the data unless `plot_range` says otherwise.

#% item
# Colours, `opacity` and `edge_form` before it in its list style it; faces are lit.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The default:

#% cell
graphics3d(sphere())
#% output
#= image("sphere.files/sphere-1.png")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Several spheres in colours:

#% cell
graphics3d([red, sphere([0, 0, 0], 1), blue, sphere([2.5, 0, 0], 0.5), green, sphere([[0, 2, 0], [2, 2, 0]], 0.4)])
#% output
#= image("sphere.files/sphere-2.png")

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A translucent sphere around a point:

#% cell
graphics3d([opacity(0.4), sphere(), opacity(1), black, point_size(large), point([0, 0, 0])])
#% output
#= image("sphere.files/sphere-3.png")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `graphics3d`, `cuboid`, `plot3d`
